How to pull files off iphone14 using rhel9 cmdline
May 21
Searched the web

The iPhone 14 can currently be on iOS 26 (that's what Apple ships now — they renumbered). The EPEL packages lag well behind; for iOS 17+ you need to build from source. Here's the full procedure:

Important caveat up front: EPEL's packaged libimobiledevice is typically old enough that it breaks on iOS 17+. Build from source. The dependency chain has a specific order that must be followed.

Step 1 — Install build deps
bash

dnf install -y epel-release
dnf install -y git gcc make autoconf automake libtool pkg-config \
    openssl-devel libusb-devel fuse3-devel usbutils
# usbmuxd from EPEL as a baseline (will be overridden by source build)
dnf install -y usbmuxd

Step 2 — Build the stack from source (order is mandatory)

Each repo must be built and installed before the next. libtatsu is a newer requirement that has no distro package yet.
bash

mkdir ~/limd && cd ~/limd

for repo in libplist libimobiledevice-glue libusbmuxd usbmuxd libtatsu libimobiledevice ifuse; do
    git clone https://github.com/libimobiledevice/${repo}.git
    cd ${repo}
    ./autogen.sh --prefix=/usr/local
    make -j$(nproc)
    make install
    cd ..
done

ldconfig

usbmuxd in that chain replaces the EPEL binary. After make install for usbmuxd, restart the service:
bash

systemctl stop usbmuxd
systemctl disable usbmuxd   # let it launch on USB hotplug instead

Step 3 — Trust the device

Plug in the iPhone. The screen will prompt "Trust This Computer" — you must tap Trust physically on the device. Then:
bash

idevicepair pair
idevicepair validate     # should say SUCCESS

If it says "Please accept the trust dialog" — unlock the phone first, then repeat. If you get SSL errors, confirm the build linked against OpenSSL, not GnuTLS (idevice_id --version output will show).

Step 4 — Verify connection
bash

idevice_id -l            # prints UDID if paired
ideviceinfo              # dumps device properties
idevicename              # prints device name

Step 5 — Mount with ifuse
bash

mkdir -p /mnt/iphone
modprobe fuse            # if not already loaded
# add yourself to fuse group or run as root
ifuse /mnt/iphone
ls /mnt/iphone/

The mount will show DCIM/, Books/, Downloads/, and app sandbox folders (if the app opts in).

Step 6 — Pull files
bash

# Photos / videos
cp -r /mnt/iphone/DCIM/ ~/iphone_photos/

# Specific file types
find /mnt/iphone/DCIM -name "*.HEIC" -exec cp {} ~/photos/ \;
find /mnt/iphone/DCIM -name "*.MOV" -exec cp {} ~/videos/ \;

# rsync is cleaner for large pulls
rsync -av --progress /mnt/iphone/DCIM/ ~/iphone_photos/

# Unmount when done
fusermount3 -u /mnt/iphone

Alternative — backup without mounting (gets more data)

idevicebackup2 can pull a full unencrypted backup including SMS, app data, etc.:
bash

idevicebackup2 backup --full /tmp/iphone_backup/

The backup is in Apple's backup format (plist + binary blobs). To read it you'd need a parser like iphone-backup-decrypt (Python) or libimobiledevice's own tools.

Known issues with iOS 17+/iOS 26:

The EPEL-packaged version will typically fail at the idevicepair pair step with a protocol error. That's why the full source build is necessary. The libtatsu library is specifically required for newer iOS versions and has no distro package yet on RHEL 9. The build chain above includes it.

If you hit No device found after pairing, check that usbmuxd is running (systemctl status usbmuxd) and that the USB cable is data-capable (some Lightning cables are charge-only — relevant since the 14 is Lightning).

To run code, enable code execution and file creation in Settings > Capabilities.

