Searched the web

Good, got the authoritative fwupd wiki page and the full picture on the KEK/DB situation. Here's the complete breakdown:

Inventor: ESP not detected — diagnosis and fix

The official cause list from fwupd's own wiki. Run this first:
bash

fwupdtool esp-list --verbose

The most common output on RHEL 9 is the UDisks error. UDisks is now mandatory — the EspLocation config workaround was deprecated and removed.
bash

# Step 1 — install udisks2 if missing
dnf install udisks2

# Step 2 — start and enable it
systemctl enable --now udisks2

# Step 3 — restart fwupd
systemctl restart fwupd

# Step 4 — verify
fwupdtool esp-list --verbose

If UDisks is running but ESP still not found, check the partition flags:
bash

lsblk -o NAME,FSTYPE,MOUNTPOINT,PARTTYPE
# The ESP partition must have partition type GUID: C12A7328-F81F-11D2-BA4B-00A0C93EC93B
# (the EFI System Partition GUID)

# Check with parted:
parted /dev/sda print
# Look for "boot, esp" flags on the FAT32 partition

If the ESP flag is missing:
bash

parted /dev/sda set 1 esp on   # replace "1" with your ESP partition number

If the partition type GUID is wrong (MBR-style type instead of GPT EFI GUID):
bash

gdisk /dev/sda
# type: t → select partition → EF00 (EFI System)
# then w to write

Also verify the ESP is mounted rw at a recognized path (/boot, /boot/efi, or /esp):
bash

mount | grep -i efi
# If not mounted:
mount /dev/sda1 /boot/efi

After any of these fixes, restart fwupd and re-run esp-list.

Inventor: KEK, UEFI db / Windows Production PCA — "no updates available"

These three showing up as Devices with no available firmware updates is expected and not a bug for most systems right now. What's happening:

The Microsoft KEK CA 2011 and Windows Production PCA 2011 are both expiring in June 2026. Microsoft has been rolling out replacement certs (KEK CA 2023, Windows UEFI CA 2023) in phases since early 2024. The new Microsoft Windows UEFI CA 2023 will be used to sign Windows boot components prior to the expiration of the Windows Production CA 2011, and the rollout began as an optional update in February 2024, with a controlled rollout to all devices continuing through 2024-2025.
Microsoft Community Hub

The reason fwupd shows "no updates available" for these is that KEK and DB are listed as not having updates available when querying via fwupdtool — this is because the LVFS distribution of these Microsoft cert updates is gated by a CHID (hardware ID) allowlist. Your specific hardware CHID has to be cleared by Dell before the update flows to your device. This is intentional — a bad KEK/DB push can softbrick a machine.
GitHub

What you can do right now:
bash

# Confirm your current cert versions (should show 2011)
fwupdmgr get-devices | grep -A5 -E "KEK|Windows Production|UEFI Signature"

# Force a metadata refresh from LVFS
fwupdmgr refresh --force

# Check if your CHID is now cleared
fwupdmgr get-updates

If updates still don't appear via LVFS, the manual path is via the Dell BIOS update — Dell embeds the new KEK/db certs inside the BIOS capsule itself. The BIOS 1.39.0 release contains security updates addressing Dell Security Advisories DSA-2025-005 and DSA-2025-021. Flashing that BIOS update (which you already have the direct link for) will likely carry the new Secure Boot certs along with the BIOS, which is the OEM's intended delivery mechanism for this hardware generation. After flashing, recheck:
Dell
bash

fwupdmgr get-devices | grep -A3 "KEK\|Windows Production"
# Version should advance from 2011 → 2023

If you need to push the cert updates independently without waiting on Dell's CHID clearance, the LVFS testing remote can be enabled to get unreleased/pre-release updates:
bash

fwupdmgr enable-remote lvfs-testing
fwupdmgr refresh
fwupdmgr get-updates

That's the only fully supported Linux path — there's no standalone .cap file for these cert updates distributed outside of LVFS or OEM BIOS bundles. Microsoft's own distribution is Windows Update only.

