It failed upon reboot but it had power in, and options checked in bios to allow it. It failed after it loaded the firmware on reboot in the update dell screen in red lettera
May 22
Searched the web

The red text on the Dell BIOS update screen is the firmware's own capsule verifier rejecting the payload. There are four known causes for this specific failure mode on the 5480, in order of likelihood:

Most likely: Secure Boot is blocking the capsule signature

The Dell BIOS update EXE embeds a UEFI capsule signed with a Dell cert. If Secure Boot's db doesn't include that cert, the firmware rejects it at apply-time with a red failure screen. This is especially likely given the current KEK/db situation — the 5480 shipped with old 2011-era Microsoft certs and the Dell signing cert may not be in the db on your specific configuration.

Fix — enter BIOS setup (F2 at POST) and check:

Security → Secure Boot → Secure Boot Enable → set to Disabled temporarily

Then re-stage the update from RHEL and reboot again. If it applies, re-enable Secure Boot after. You can also try:

Security → Secure Boot → Secure Boot Mode → set to Audit Mode or Deployed Mode

Some 5480s have a specific "Allow BIOS Downgrade" and "UEFI Capsule Firmware Updates" toggle under:

Maintenance → BIOS Recovery and Update → UEFI Capsule Firmware Updates → Enabled

Verify that's on — it's separate from the general update permission you already set.

Second: Version jump too large — intermediate version required

Dell sometimes blocks direct jumps between non-adjacent versions — for example, going from 1.15 to 1.16 may require installing 1.15.1 first, with no indication of this requirement unless you read the readme.
Dell

Check your current version first:
bash

fwupdmgr get-devices | grep -A3 "System Firmware"
# or:
dmidecode -s bios-version

If you're on something old like 1.x and trying to jump directly to 1.39.0, you may need a stepping-stone version. The 5480 readme for 1.39.0 at Dell's site will list the minimum previous version. The intermediate version most commonly needed for this platform is somewhere in the 1.19–1.22 range.

Third: Intel ME version mismatch

On Dell platforms, if the system has been powered on continuously for more than approximately 24 days without an AC power cycle, the Intel Management Engine fails to enter recovery mode for the BIOS update — the first attempt fails, and only after the failed attempt issues a global reset does a second attempt succeed.
Wikipedia

Full AC power cycle fix:

    Shut down completely
    Unplug AC
    Hold power button 15 seconds (drains capacitors)
    Reconnect AC, boot, re-stage update via fwupd, reboot

Fourth: Check update result log

After the failed reboot, fwupd records the outcome:
bash

fwupdmgr get-results <device-id>
# get device-id from: fwupdmgr get-devices

This will give the exact error code the firmware returned. Common ones:
Code	Meaning
UEFI_CAPSULE_RESULT_UNSUCCESSFUL	Generic rejection — Secure Boot or ME issue
UEFI_CAPSULE_RESULT_OUT_OF_RESOURCES	ESP too small or NVRAM full
UEFI_CAPSULE_RESULT_NOT_FOUND	Capsule not staged correctly — ESP mount issue
UEFI_CAPSULE_RESULT_SECURITY_VIOLATION	Signature verification failed — Secure Boot cert mismatch

The SECURITY_VIOLATION result points directly at the Secure Boot path. Post the get-results output and the exact red text you saw if you can recall it, and we can pin it exactly.

