Need to quickly resign rhel9 certs from the os, so I can turn secure boot back on and use tpm properly
May 23
Searched the web

Here's the full procedure. On RHEL 9 with Red Hat's signed shim already in place, you don't need to re-sign the shim or grub — Red Hat handles that chain. What you need is a MOK key pair to cover anything outside that chain (custom modules, third-party drivers) and to satisfy TPM PCR measurements.

Step 1 — Install required tools
bash

dnf install -y pesign openssl mokutil keyutils kernel-devel

Step 2 — Check current state
bash

# Confirm Secure Boot is currently off
mokutil --sb-state

# See what's already enrolled
mokutil --list-enrolled

# Check existing boot chain signatures
pesign -S -i /boot/efi/EFI/redhat/shimx64.efi
pesign -S -i /boot/efi/EFI/redhat/grubx64.efi
pesign -S -i /boot/vmlinuz-$(uname -r)

If all three show valid Red Hat signatures you're good — the RH shim chain is intact and Secure Boot will work as-is. The most common case after the BIOS update failure is that the shim is fine but a custom module or the BIOS flash left something unsigned.

Step 3 — Generate your MOK key pair
bash

mkdir -p /root/secureboot-keys
cd /root/secureboot-keys

openssl req -new -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 \
  -keyout MOK.priv \
  -outform DER \
  -out MOK.der \
  -noenc \
  -days 3650 \
  -subj "/CN=$(hostname) Secure Boot MOK/"

# Also make a PEM copy for sbsign
openssl x509 -in MOK.der -inform DER -out MOK.pem -outform PEM

chmod 600 /root/secureboot-keys/MOK.priv

Step 4 — Enroll the MOK key
bash

mokutil --import /root/secureboot-keys/MOK.der
# Enter a one-time enrollment password — you'll need this at the next boot

Reboot. Before GRUB loads, the blue MokManager screen will appear:

Select: Enroll MOK → Continue → Yes → enter your password → Reboot

Step 5 — Sign anything that needs it

The stock RHEL 9 kernel, shim, and grub are already signed by Red Hat. You only need to sign things you built or added:
bash

# Sign a kernel module
/usr/src/kernels/$(uname -r)/scripts/sign-file sha256 \
  /root/secureboot-keys/MOK.priv \
  /root/secureboot-keys/MOK.der \
  /path/to/module.ko

# Sign a custom kernel or EFI binary
sbsign --key /root/secureboot-keys/MOK.priv \
       --cert /root/secureboot-keys/MOK.pem \
       --output /boot/vmlinuz-custom.signed \
       /boot/vmlinuz-custom

Step 6 — Enable Secure Boot in BIOS, then verify

After re-enabling in BIOS Setup (F2 → Security → Secure Boot → Enabled):
bash

# Confirm it's on
mokutil --sb-state
# Expected: SecureBoot enabled

# Check MOK is enrolled
mokutil --list-enrolled | grep -A3 "$(hostname)"

# Verify kernel is booting with RH chain intact
dmesg | grep -i "secure boot"
dmesg | grep -i "lockdown"

Step 7 — Wire TPM to Secure Boot via PCR policy

Once Secure Boot is confirmed on:
bash

# Check TPM is visible
ls /dev/tpm*
tpm2_getcap properties-fixed | grep TPMVendorID

# Check PCR values (PCR7 = Secure Boot state — should now be non-zero)
tpm2_pcrread sha256:0,1,7

# If using LUKS with TPM binding:
systemd-cryptenroll --tpm2-device=auto \
  --tpm2-pcrs=0+1+7 \
  /dev/sdaX   # your LUKS partition

PCR7 specifically reflects Secure Boot policy — it's zero when Secure Boot is off and gets a proper measurement when on, which is what lets TPM-bound secrets unseal correctly.

If grub or shim signatures are actually broken (pesign showed errors in Step 2):
bash

# Reinstall the signed versions from RH
dnf reinstall shim-x64 grub2-efi-x64

# Rebuild grub config
grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/efi/EFI/redhat/grub.cfg

That pulls fresh Red Hat-signed EFI binaries from the repo, restoring the trust chain without touching your keys.

