The picture is clear. This is almost certainly the SBAT revocation issue — on 20 August 2024, a Windows patch revoked shims older than version 15.8, which writes a new SBAT policy into UEFI NVRAM that blocks older shims even on Linux-only machines if the BIOS has received the updated dbx. The blue screen with "SBAT self-check failed" or "Verification failed" is the symptom.
Dell

Here's the full fix sequence, Secure Boot stays off until the very last step:

Step 1 — Diagnose exactly what you have
bash

# Check shim version installed
rpm -q shim-x64

# Check current SBAT revocations burned into NVRAM
mokutil --list-sbat-revocations

# Check what the shim EFI binary itself claims
pesign -S -i /boot/efi/EFI/redhat/shimx64.efi | grep -i sbat

If shim is older than 15.8-2.el9 that's your root cause.

Step 2 — Update shim and grub together (mandatory — do not update one without the other)
bash

dnf update shim-x64 shim grub2-efi-x64 grub2-common mokutil

# Confirm you now have 15.8
rpm -q shim-x64
# Expected: shim-x64-15.8-2.el9 or newer

Step 3 — Clear the bad SBAT policy from NVRAM

The Windows patch wrote a SBAT revocation into UEFI NVRAM that blocks the old shim. Even after updating shim the NVRAM policy may still reject it on the next boot. Clear it first:
bash

mokutil --set-sbat-policy delete

Then reboot once back into RHEL (still with Secure Boot off) to let the updated shim write fresh SBAT data:
bash

systemctl reboot

Boot back in, then confirm the SBAT policy has been refreshed:
bash

mokutil --list-sbat-revocations
# Should now show shim,4 or higher, not the old revocation entries

Step 4 — Reinstall shim EFI binaries to ESP

Sometimes the ESP copy doesn't get updated even when the RPM does:
bash

# Confirm current shim on ESP matches the new package
ls -la /boot/efi/EFI/redhat/shimx64.efi
rpm -ql shim-x64 | grep efi

# Force reinstall to push new binary to ESP
dnf reinstall shim-x64 shim

# Rebuild grub config
grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/efi/EFI/redhat/grub.cfg

Step 5 — Verify the boot chain before re-enabling Secure Boot
bash

pesign -S -i /boot/efi/EFI/redhat/shimx64.efi
pesign -S -i /boot/efi/EFI/redhat/grubx64.efi
pesign -S -i /boot/vmlinuz-$(uname -r)

All three must show valid Red Hat signatures. If grub or the kernel shows unsigned, reinstall:
bash

dnf reinstall grub2-efi-x64 kernel-$(uname -r)

Step 6 — Re-enable Secure Boot in BIOS

Go into BIOS Setup (F2), re-enable Secure Boot, save and exit. On first boot you should get straight to GRUB with no blue screen.

Confirm:
bash

mokutil --sb-state
# SecureBoot enabled

dmesg | grep -i "secure boot"
# Should show: Secure boot enabled

If you still get the blue screen after all of this, the BIOS itself has the old revocation baked into dbx rather than just NVRAM. Check:
bash

dbxtool -l
# or
efi-readvar -v dbx | grep -i shim

If the dbx contains a shim 15.7 revocation hash, it needs to be cleared from the BIOS side — go into BIOS Setup → Security → Secure Boot → Reset to Factory Keys / Delete all Secure Boot variables, then re-enroll the platform keys fresh. That wipes the bad dbx entry.

