Content was not encrypted, content is detached, signature cannot be verified on after reinstall kernel grub-efi and pesign check
May 23
Searched the web

Good news first: "Content was not encrypted. Content is detached; signature cannot be verified" is normal output from pesign -S — it is not an error. This is the standard output when checking signatures on signed EFI binaries like grubx64.efi — the "detached" message means the signature is stored separately from the content it covers, which is how PE/COFF Authenticode signing works for all EFI binaries. Every correctly signed Red Hat EFI binary produces this exact output.
tomsguide

What you actually need to check is what comes with that message — the signer's common name. Run this:
bash

pesign -S -i /boot/efi/EFI/redhat/shimx64.efi
pesign -S -i /boot/efi/EFI/redhat/grubx64.efi
pesign -S -i /boot/vmlinuz-$(uname -r)

Good output looks like:

Content was not encrypted.
Content is detached; signature cannot be verified.
The signer's common name is Red Hat Secure Boot (CA key 1)
The signer's email address is secalert@redhat.com
Signing time: ...
There were certs or crls included.

Bad output (actually unsigned) looks like:

No signatures found.

So paste what the signer's common name line says for each of the three files and we'll know exactly where the chain is broken before moving to shim. The "cannot be verified" wording is pesign's way of saying it can't do full chain validation locally — that's intentional and expected. The UEFI firmware does the actual cryptographic verification at boot time against the keys in its db.

