The dbxupdate.bin from uefi.org is a raw EFI signature list but needs to be wrapped in an authenticated variable update structure (.auth format) before the BIOS will accept it. Dell's Custom mode BIOS importer expects .auth not raw .bin.
Searched the web

Two important findings from that search:

    The dbxupdate_x64.bin file from the UEFI Forum is actually already a signed signature list (.auth format) — just with a .bin filename extension. So renaming it may be all you need for the BIOS browser to accept it.
    Debian
    The easier path entirely: sbkeysync can apply the raw .bin directly to the live dbx EFI variable from Linux without touching the BIOS at all — but that requires the efivars path to exist, which yours doesn't because dbx is empty.
    Fedora Project

So two approaches, pick whichever fits:

Option A — Rename to .auth and retry BIOS import (simplest)
bash

cp /tmp/dbxupdate.bin /boot/efi/dbxupdate.auth

# Then in BIOS Custom mode:
# DBX → Import → browse ESP → select dbxupdate.auth

The .auth extension is what Dell's BIOS browser filters for. The file content is identical — just the extension tells the browser to treat it as an authenticated variable update.

Option B — Apply from Linux using efi-updatevar directly

Since dbx is empty the efivars node doesn't exist yet — but efi-updatevar can create it:
bash

dnf install -y efitools

# This creates the dbx variable from scratch
efi-updatevar -f /tmp/dbxupdate.bin dbx

# Verify
dbxtool -l | wc -l

If that fails with "no such file" on the efivars path, force-create it:
bash

# Make sure efivars is mounted and writable
mount | grep efivars
mount -o remount,rw /sys/firmware/efi/efivars

# Remove immutable flag if set
chattr -i /sys/firmware/efi/efivars/dbx-d719b2cb-3d3a-4596-a3bc-dad00e67656f 2>/dev/null

# Try again
efi-updatevar -f /tmp/dbxupdate.bin dbx

Option C — KeyTool.efi (nuclear fallback if both above fail)
bash

dnf install -y efitools
cp /usr/share/efitools/efi/KeyTool.efi /boot/efi/KeyTool.efi
cp /tmp/dbxupdate.bin /boot/efi/dbxupdate.auth

# Add it as a one-time boot option
efibootmgr --create --disk /dev/sda --part 1 \
  --label "KeyTool" \
  --loader '\KeyTool.efi'

systemctl reboot
# Boot into KeyTool → Edit Keys → The dbx → Add → select dbxupdate.auth

KeyTool runs before the OS and talks to EFI firmware directly, bypassing the OS efivars path entirely. This works even when the variable is missing.

