# power cycle the device and watch what IP it ARPs from

SSH version mismatch — fix on RHEL 9:

The "built against / you have" error is an OpenSSH version negotiation failure. The router is running an old Dropbear or OpenSSH that only offers deprecated algorithms. Fix it on the client side without touching the server:
bash

# Try connecting with legacy algorithm support explicitly
ssh -o KexAlgorithms=+diffie-hellman-group1-sha1 \
    -o HostKeyAlgorithms=+ssh-rsa \
    -o PubkeyAcceptedKeyTypes=+ssh-rsa \
    -o Ciphers=+aes128-cbc,3des-cbc \
    root@192.168.1.1

If that works, make it permanent for this host in ~/.ssh/config:
bash

cat >> ~/.ssh/config << 'EOF'
Host 192.168.1.1
    KexAlgorithms +diffie-hellman-group1-sha1,diffie-hellman-group14-sha1
    HostKeyAlgorithms +ssh-rsa
    PubkeyAcceptedKeyTypes +ssh-rsa
    Ciphers +aes128-cbc,3des-cbc,aes256-cbc
    User root
EOF

Then just:
bash

ssh 192.168.1.1

The reason this happens is RHEL 9's OpenSSH disables legacy algorithms by default for security. The router's embedded SSH (usually Dropbear) predates the modern algorithm set so the two ends can't agree on a cipher without explicitly re-enabling the old ones on the client.

