The "pipe 3" in ping output means 3 packets were in flight when you cancelled — destination unreachable confirms it's not responding at layer 3 at all. The device is either not fully booted, on a completely different subnet, or the port you're on isn't active.

Try every possible access method:

Physical port rotation — try them all

The AX89X has multiple physical interfaces and depending on what firmware state it's in, only one may be active:
bash

# For each port you try, set your IP and test:
ip addr flush dev <iface>
ip addr add 192.168.1.2/24 dev <iface>
ip link set <iface> up
arping -I <iface> 192.168.1.1    # ARP is lower level than ping, works even if ICMP blocked

Try in this order:

    Yellow LAN ports (any one of the four)
    Blue 1G WAN port
    10GbE port
    SFP+ (if you have the transceiver)

Scan every common prplOS/OpenWrt subnet:
bash

# Try all common default subnets one by one
for ip in 192.168.1.1 192.168.0.1 192.168.2.1 10.0.0.1 10.10.0.1; do
    ip addr flush dev <iface>
    ip addr add ${ip%.*}.2/24 dev <iface>
    ip link set <iface> up
    sleep 1
    arping -c 3 -I <iface> $ip 2>/dev/null && echo "FOUND: $ip" || echo "dead: $ip"
done

