Good clarification. You want a hardened passthrough — WAN gets internet traffic and nothing else, ever. Let me address each layer properly.

On disabling peripherals — the right answer:

    RTC (qcom,pm8941-rtc) — keep enabled. Needed for log timestamps and time-based security policies. Disabling breaks forensics.
    MMC/SDHCI (qcom,sdhci-msm-v4) — disable if no SD card used. Removes a block device attack surface.
    SPI (qcom,spi-qup-v2.2.1) — keep if it's the NOR flash bus. Disabling bricks the device. Check which devices are on it first.
    I2C (qcom,i2c-qup-v2.2.1) — the ti,lp5562 is an LED controller on I2C. Disable if you don't need LED indicators. Otherwise keep.
    EDMA (qcom,edma) — do NOT disable. This is the Ethernet DMA engine. Disabling kills all network traffic including LAN.
    MDIO — do NOT disable. This is the PHY management bus. Disabling kills PHY negotiation, ports go dead.
    DMA controllers — keep. Required for NSS packet processing.
    Timers — only disable high-resolution timers not used by the network stack. The NSS data plane needs precise scheduling.

