Fix — add sda3 to it:
bash

# Option 1 — use lvmdevices to add it properly
lvmdevices --adddev /dev/sda3

# Verify it was added
cat /etc/lvm/devices/system.devices

# Now scan and activate
pvscan --cache -aay /dev/sda3
vgchange -aay
lvs

If lvmdevices command missing or fails:
bash

# Option 2 — add manually
# Get sda3 PVID
pvck --dump headers /dev/sda3 2>&1 | grep PVID

# Add entry to devices file
PVID=$(pvck --dump headers /dev/sda3 2>&1 | \
  grep -i pvid | awk '{print $NF}')

echo "IDTYPE=sys_wwid IDNAME=/dev/sda3 DEVNAME=/dev/sda3 PVID=$PVID" \
  >> /etc/lvm/devices/system.devices

cat /etc/lvm/devices/system.devices

Or nuclear option — disable the devices file entirely:
bash

# Tell LVM to ignore devices file and scan everything
sed -i 's/use_devicesfile = 1/use_devicesfile = 0/' \
  /etc/lvm/lvm.conf

# Or add to lvm.conf devices section
echo 'devices { use_devicesfile = 0 }' \
  >> /etc/lvm/lvm.conf

# Retry
vgscan
vgchange -aay
lvs
ls -la /dev/mapper/

The use_devicesfile = 0 option is the fastest fix — bypasses the whole devices file mechanism and goes back to scanning all block devices like older LVM versions did. Paste cat /etc/lvm/devices/system.devices so we see what's in there.

