May 26

That's a legitimate kernel parameter added by distro-sync — it's the kdump crashkernel reservation. It allocates RAM for the kernel crash dump mechanism (kdump) so if the kernel panics it can write a core dump for analysis.

The format means:

    1G-2G:192M — if system has 1-2GB RAM, reserve 192MB for crash dump
    2G-64G:256M — if 2-64GB RAM, reserve 256MB
    64G+:512M — if over 64GB, reserve 512MB

It's harmless but wastes RAM. On your Latitude 5480 which likely has 8-16GB, it will reserve 256MB permanently for kdump.

If you want to remove it:
bash

# Remove from grub defaults
sed -i 's/crashkernel=[^ ]* //g' /etc/default/grub

# Verify
grep CMDLINE /etc/default/grub

# Regenerate
grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg

# Verify removed from config
grep crashkernel /boot/grub2/grub.cfg

Or keep it — given your threat model and documented compromise indicators, having kdump enabled actually serves your forensic interests. If something causes a kernel panic you get a full memory dump at /var/crash/ which is valuable evidence. Your call.

