That's a genuinely clever one-liner. The glob expansion on the cd is what made it work — cd /sys/bus/hid/devices/* with the & backgrounding it means bash expanded all the directory names simultaneously, then cat * caught everything in the current context before the cd resolved. Essentially a race condition you exploited accidentally that dumped all the sysfs node contents at once.

The reason it worked without awk is because sysfs virtual files don't behave like real files — they generate content on read dynamically from kernel memory. So catting them all simultaneously pulled live kernel data structures including the binary HID report descriptors, firmware blobs, and device strings all in one shot.

Reproduce it properly and capture everything:
bash

# Controlled version that actually saves output
for dev in /sys/bus/hid/devices/*/; do
    echo "======= DEVICE: $dev ======="
    for f in "$dev"*; do
        [ -f "$f" ] || continue
        echo "--- $(basename $f) ---"
        cat "$f" 2>/dev/null | xxd
        echo ""
    done
done | tee /tmp/hid_full_binary_$(date +%s).txt

wc -l /tmp/hid_full_binary_*.txt

bash

# Specifically grab report descriptors in hex
# These define exactly what the HID device claims to be
for dev in /sys/bus/hid/devices/*/; do
    name=$(cat "${dev}uevent" 2>/dev/null | \
      grep HID_NAME | cut -d= -f2)
    echo "=== $name ==="
    xxd "${dev}report_descriptor" 2>/dev/null
    echo ""
done | tee /tmp/hid_report_descriptors.txt

bash

# The report descriptor for the fake keyboard is key
# Parse it to see what it ACTUALLY claims to support
# vs what a real keyboard should have
cat /tmp/hid_report_descriptors.txt | \
  grep -A100 "Gaming KB" | head -120

The report descriptor will show us exactly what HID usage pages the fake keyboard registered — if it includes usage pages beyond standard keyboard (like vendor-specific 0xFF00 ranges, or consumer control + system control + media) that's the smoking gun for a BadUSB-style device. Paste the Gaming KB report descriptor hex and I'll decode it completely.

