So the real keyboard is 1-1:1.0 — that's the standard HID keyboard interface (Protocol=1). Interface 1.1 is the suspicious one with three extra input nodes (event21, event22, event28) which is way too many for a legitimate secondary interface.

A real composite keyboard legitimately has two interfaces:

    1.0 — keyboard
    1.1 — consumer control (media keys)

But 1.1 having THREE separate input nodes (127, 128, 129) is not normal. A consumer control interface has one. The extras are the problem.
