Replace mmcblk0p1 with your actual SD card boot partition once you confirm it.

2GB SD card partition layout — absolute minimum for FIPS:

FIPS mandates a separate unencrypted /boot. On 2GB this is the only viable layout:

/boot/efi   200MB   vfat    (if UEFI)
/boot       512MB   xfs     (FIPS requires separate /boot, unencrypted)
/           ~1.2GB  xfs     (everything else)
swap        0       none    (no room — add zram post-install)

No LVM — not enough space. Straight partitions only.

Redirect package cache to sdc — kickstart method:

Create this kickstart on sdc before booting, or paste it into Anaconda's kickstart URL field:
bash

# Write this to sdc1 before booting
cat > /mnt/sdc/ks.cfg << 'EOF'
text
lang en_US.UTF-8
keyboard us
timezone America/Los_Angeles --utc

# FIPS on bootloader
bootloader --location=mbr --append="fips=1"

# Minimal packages only — 2GB is tight
%packages --inst-langs=en --excludedocs
@^minimal-environment
crypto-policies
crypto-policies-scripts
-dracut-config-rescue
-microcode_ctl
-NetworkManager-tui
-parted
%end

# Redirect ALL dnf cache and data to sdc
# SD card stays read-only after install
%post --erroronfail
# Mount sdc for persistent package storage
mkdir -p /mnt/packages

# Configure dnf to use sdc for everything
cat >> /etc/dnf/dnf.conf << 'DNFEOF'
cachedir=/mnt/packages/dnf/cache
persistdir=/mnt/packages/dnf/persist
logdir=/mnt/packages/dnf/log
DNFEOF

# Mount sdc at boot for package operations
echo "/dev/sdc1 /mnt/packages xfs defaults,nofail 0 2" \
  >> /etc/fstab

# Make /var/cache/dnf a symlink to sdc
rm -rf /var/cache/dnf
ln -sf /mnt/packages/dnf/cache /var/cache/dnf

# Enable FIPS crypto policy
update-crypto-policies --set FIPS

# Lock root — Rocky 10 disables by default anyway
passwd -l root

# Make / read-only on next boot
# Add ro flag to grub
sed -i 's/GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX="/GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX="ro /' \
  /etc/default/grub

# Redirect writes to tmpfs for read-only operation
cat >> /etc/fstab << 'FSEOF'
tmpfs /tmp tmpfs defaults,noatime,size=64m 0 0
tmpfs /var/log tmpfs defaults,noatime,size=32m 0 0
tmpfs /var/tmp tmpfs defaults,noatime,size=32m 0 0
FSEOF

grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg
%end

reboot
EOF

Then add inst.ks=hd:/dev/sdc1:/ks.cfg to the GRUB line:

fips=1 inst.text inst.geoloc=0 inst.ks=hd:/dev/sdc1:/ks.cfg inst.nompath inst.nodmraid

Important Rocky 10 changes that affect you:

Rocky Linux 10 disables the root account by default — system management requires creating an administrative user with full sudo privileges. The graphical interface no longer supports adding third-party repositories during initial installation — use inst.addrepo boot option or kickstart instead.
Wikipedia

So add to your kickstart:
