Device side:

Physical keys → ATtiny85 → MAX232 level shifter → DB9 serial

Serial has no enumeration, no DMA, no driver loading cascade. It's bytes on a wire. The attack surface is essentially zero.

Option 4 — The cleanest: I2C with cryptographic authentication:

Trusted input device
→ STM32 MCU with BLAKE3 HMAC on every keystroke
→ I2C bus to host
→ Host driver validates HMAC before accepting keycode
→ Any injected keystroke without valid HMAC is dropped

Every keypress is signed. Your CTW-11 BLAKE3 KDF infrastructure already handles this. The host maintains a shared secret burned into the MCU's OTP fuses at manufacture. Injected keystrokes without the secret are cryptographically impossible to forge.

The firmware design for Option 4:
c

// MCU side — pseudocode
#include "blake3.h"

uint8_t otp_secret[32];  // burned to OTP fuse, unreadable
uint32_t counter = 0;    // monotonic, prevents replay

void send_keycode(uint8_t keycode) {
    uint8_t msg[37];
    msg[0] = keycode;
    memcpy(msg + 1, &counter, 4);
    // HMAC = BLAKE3(secret || counter || keycode)
    blake3_keyed_hash(otp_secret, msg, 5, msg + 5);
    i2c_send(msg, 37);
    counter++;
}

