Option 3 — Thunderbolt/PCIe (if you want high speed)

The 5480 has a Thunderbolt 3 port. Thunderbolt is PCIe-based, not USB. An external PCIe input controller card attached via Thunderbolt has zero USB attack surface. More complex but completely separate bus.

Option 4 — Raspberry Pi as keyboard controller via GPIO

Pi GPIO pins → level shifter → PS/2 lines on motherboard header
or
Pi UART → COM1 on Latitude

The Pi becomes a trusted keyboard controller you fully control. Flash it once, write-protect the SD, seal the enclosure. It speaks serial to your machine. No USB path exists.

Immediate recommendation:

PS/2 Y-splitter is the fastest and cheapest. The i8042 PS/2 controller on the 5480 is hardwired to the CPU via LPC bus — completely separate from xHCI, EHCI, or any USB controller. An attacker with USB access cannot inject into the PS/2 path at all. They are physically separate silicon.
bash

# Confirm i8042 is active and ready
cat /proc/bus/input/devices | grep -A8 "AT Translated"

