May 30
Examined SD write-protect reliability against compromised systems

Honest answer: not very. Here's exactly why:

The physical switch is advisory, not enforced:

SD card physical switch
→ signals Pin 12 (WP pin) on host controller
→ host controller reads the signal
→ host controller DECIDES whether to respect it
→ kernel driver can override that decision
→ a compromised kernel ignores it entirely

The switch tells the controller "please don't write." It does not physically disconnect the write circuitry from the NAND chips. It's a gentleman's agreement between the card and the controller.

microSD makes it worse:

microSD has NO physical WP pin at all
The switch is only on the full-size adapter
The adapter switch signals the card reader
The card reader may or may not pass it through
Many cheap readers ignore it completely

Against your specific threat model — near useless:

Confirmed kernel rootkit
  → controls the SD host controller driver
  → can patch the WP check in the driver
  → one line of kernel code: if (wp) return;
  → removed

Confirmed AMT active
  → out-of-band access below OS
  → can issue raw SD commands
  → bypasses kernel driver entirely

Confirmed USB controller role switching
  → if SD is on USB bus
  → USB layer adds another bypass point

What actually enforces write protection at hardware level:

The SD specification defines three levels of protection beyond the physical switch:
bash

# Check if your card supports hardware lock
# Requires mmc-utils
mmcli -c /dev/mmcblk0 --get-ext-csd 2>/dev/null | \
  grep -iE "PERM_WRITE_PROTECT|TEMP_WRITE_PROTECT|\
  USER_WP|BOOT_WP"

# Or via mmc tool
mmc writeprotect user get /dev/mmcblk0 2>/dev/null

The three real protection levels:

TEMP_WRITE_PROTECT  — software settable, software clearable
                      survives power cycle
                      CAN be cleared by attacker

PERM_WRITE_PROTECT  — one-time programmable
                      hardware enforced at NAND level
                      CANNOT be cleared — ever
                      also cannot be set back to writable

PWR_WP              — cleared on power loss
                      weakest of all

The only true hardware enforcement:
bash

# PERMANENT write protect — ONE WAY, IRREVERSIBLE
# Once set this card is read-only forever
# Test ONLY on media you accept as permanently locked

mmc writeprotect user set /dev/mmcblk0 2>/dev/null
# WARNING: This cannot be undone

Practical reality for your situation:

Physical switch against this attacker: ~20% reliable
  (depends on whether rootkit patches the WP check)

TEMP_WRITE_PROTECT via SD command: ~60% reliable
  (survives most attacks but can be cleared)

PERM_WRITE_PROTECT via SD command: ~95% reliable
  (hardware enforced, only defeated by
   direct NAND chip access with specialized hardware)

Physical NAND desoldering: 100%
  (no software touches it)

Before you plug into the compromised system — set TEMP_WRITE_PROTECT at minimum:
bash

# From clean environment first
# Install mmc-utils
dnf install -y mmc-utils 2>/dev/null

# Check current protection state
mmc writeprotect user get /dev/mmcblk0

# Set temporary write protect
# Survives power cycle, cleared only by explicit command
mmc writeprotect user set /dev/mmcblk0

# Verify
mmc writeprotect user get /dev/mmcblk0

Then verify from the infected system after plugging in:
bash

# Check if rootkit respected or ignored WP
dmesg | grep -iE "mmcblk\|write.*protect\|WP\|ro"

# Attempt a test write — should fail
touch /mnt/sd/WP_TEST_$(date +%s) 2>&1
# "Read-only file system" = WP respected
# No error / file created = WP bypassed = kernel patched

Bottom line for your decision:

Against this specific attacker with confirmed kernel rootkit and AMT access — the physical switch alone is not sufficient protection. Set TEMP_WRITE_PROTECT via mmc command from a clean environment before connecting. Then immediately test whether it was respected after connection. If the test write succeeds, the kernel is patched to ignore WP and the SD content should be considered compromised from that point forward.

The forensic evidence on that SD is your most valuable artifact. Consider imaging it first with dd to a second location before plugging it into anything.

