will it not read if i remove or obstruct the write pads?
May 30

Yes. That is actually the most reliable hardware enforcement available without specialized equipment.

The SD card pinout:

Full size SD:
Pin 1  - DAT3/CS
Pin 2  - CMD/DI
Pin 3  - VSS (GND)
Pin 4  - VDD (Power)
Pin 5  - CLK
Pin 6  - VSS (GND)
Pin 7  - DAT0/DO
Pin 8  - DAT1
Pin 9  - DAT2

microSD:
Pin 1  - DAT2
Pin 2  - DAT3/CS
Pin 3  - CMD
Pin 4  - VDD
Pin 5  - CLK
Pin 6  - VSS
Pin 7  - DAT0
Pin 8  - DAT1

There is no dedicated write pin. Write operations use the same CMD and DAT lines as read operations. You cannot obstruct writes without also obstructing reads — they share the same physical conductors.

What you CAN do physically:

Option A — Remove the WP pad contact entirely (full size SD only):

The physical switch on a full-size SD adapter connects a spring contact to Pin 12 on the host side. You can:

1. Open the SD adapter plastic shell
2. Find the small metal spring contact
   for the WP switch position
3. Bend it permanently to the LOCK position
4. Glue or tape it in place
5. Reassemble

This makes the physical switch signal permanent — but as discussed, a patched kernel ignores it.

Option B — Epoxy the connector pins (destructive, read-only forever):

Fill CMD pin (Pin 2 on full SD) with epoxy
→ Card cannot receive any commands
→ Cannot read OR write
→ Card is now a paperweight
Not useful

Option C — What actually works: obstruct CMD selectively:

The SD protocol requires CMD for both read and write. But there is an asymmetry:

READ sequence:
  Host → CMD → Card (READ command)
  Card → DAT → Host (data flows out)

WRITE sequence:
  Host → CMD → Card (WRITE command)
  Host → DAT → Card (data flows in)

If you could block current flow into the DAT pins but allow flow out — you could theoretically allow reads but block writes. In practice this requires a diode array soldered inline which is beyond field modification.

Option D — The most practical physical protection:

1. Copy the SD contents to a second SD
2. Set PERM_WRITE_PROTECT on the original
   via mmc command (irreversible)
3. The original is now a hardware-enforced
   read-only forensic artifact forever
4. Work from the copy

bash

# Image the SD first — preserve everything
dd if=/dev/mmcblk0 \
   of=/tmp/sd_image_$(date +%s).img \
   bs=4M status=progress 2>&1

sha256sum /tmp/sd_image_*.img > \
  /tmp/sd_image_*.sha256

# Then set permanent write protect on original
mmc writeprotect user set /dev/mmcblk0

Option E — Air gap via adapter modification:

Take a full-size SD adapter. With a razor blade or fine sandpaper:

Locate the gold contact pads on the adapter
The adapter has 9 contact pads
Pads are: GND VDD CLK CMD DAT0 DAT1 DAT2 DAT3

Scrape/abrade the CMD pad (Pin 2) slightly
→ Increases resistance on CMD line
→ May cause write command timeouts
→ While reads still succeed on DAT0
→ Unreliable — not recommended for evidence

Honest recommendation:

The physical modification path doesn't give you reliable read-without-write. The SD protocol doesn't have hardware separation between read and write paths at the connector level.

Your best options in order of reliability:

1. mmc PERM_WRITE_PROTECT command
   → Hardware enforced at NAND controller level
   → Survives rootkit, survives AMT
   → Irreversible — do this on the original
   → Image it first

2. Keep SD physically disconnected
   → Can't attack what isn't plugged in
   → Connect only when needed
   → Disconnect immediately after

3. Hash verification immediately on mount
   → Detect if writes occurred despite WP
   → Compare against known manifest
   → Automated:

   mount -o ro /dev/mmcblk0p2 /mnt/sd
   sha256sum $(find /mnt/sd -type f | sort) | \
     diff - /tmp/sd_image_*.sha256 && \
     echo "CLEAN" || echo "MODIFIED — DISCONNECT NOW"

The hash check on mount is actually your strongest tool here — it doesn't prevent writes but it detects them within seconds of connection, before the attacker can do meaningful damage. Pair it with immediate unmount on any hash mismatch and you have a functional integrity check even against a kernel that ignores WP signals.

