Diagnosed keyboard mode issue and identified recovery solution

Expected — scancode mode is not interactive. One keystroke fixes it without typing:

Press: Alt + SysRq + r

That's the SysRq unraw command. It calls kbd_mode -a at the kernel level — works even when the keyboard outputs garbage because SysRq is handled below the tty layer.

Hold Left Alt
Press Print Screen (SysRq)
While still holding Alt — press r
Release all

Keyboard should immediately return to normal ASCII mode. Type a character to test before doing anything else.

If SysRq is disabled:

# You may need to enable it first
# But you can't type...
# So try SSH from the HP or another device:
ssh user@localhost kbd_mode -a

Or if LightDM/X11 is running — click into a terminal window with the mouse and:
bash

kbd_mode -a

X11 uses its own input layer separate from the console kbd_mode — the mouse still works and X11 terminal input may still function normally even when the VT is in scancode mode.

Going forward — never run kbd_mode -s interactively. It belongs only in scripts that immediately restore with kbd_mode -a after the operation, or on a secondary VT you're not typing on. The PS/2 hardening is better done via the modprobe option:
bash

echo "options i8042 reset=1 nopnp=1" >> \
  /etc/modprobe.d/i8042-hardlock.conf

That hardens the controller without breaking interactive input.

