The clean slate approach:

1. Policy reinstalled from RPM    ← known good baseline
2. Filesystem relabeled           ← removes attacker labels
3. Enforcing mode                 ← no bypass
4. Booleans hardened              ← attack surface reduced
5. User confinement               ← uid 1000 = staff_u
6. Root confinement               ← sysadm_r only
7. Custom neverallow module       ← ptrace/execmem/execstack
8. Unlabeled files found/fixed    ← no hiding in gaps
9. Audit monitoring               ← evidence pipeline
10. Autorelabel at boot           ← clean on every restart

The neverallow rules are the most important addition:

neverallow domain domain:process ptrace
  → No process may inspect any other
  → Kills debugger-based rootkit injection
  → Kills /proc/pid/mem write attacks

neverallow domain domain:process execmem
  → No heap execution
  → Kills shellcode in malloc'd memory
  → Standard exploit mitigation

neverallow domain domain:process execstack
  → No stack execution
  → Kills stack smashing shellcode
  → Combined with slab_nomerge in kernel
  → Memory exploitation near impossible

Paste sestatus and getsebool deny_ptrace global_ssp after running — those two confirm SELinux is enforcing and the critical protections are active.

